# A settings surface is part of the app, not a feature request

The owner reviewed the factory's first live app, `apps/2026-09-26-teacher-gradebook`, on 2026-09-30
and said (translated) *"settings — well, I guess they should have been added."* Then: **do not repair
that app — make sure the next apps cannot repeat it.** That app is frozen, and this rule is what
replaces it.

The measured starting position, re-measured in this tree:

- `scaffold/android-compose/app/src/main/java/com/gexetr/scaffold/ui/` contains exactly
  `PlayKilnApp.kt`, `about/AboutScreen.kt`, `home/{HomeScreen,HomeUiState,HomeViewModel}.kt` and
  `theme/{Color,Theme,Type}.kt` — **no settings surface of any kind**, and every shipped slot copied
  that shape. Nothing let the user change a theme, a language, or any preference.
- The scaffold's `gradle/libs.versions.toml` declared `datastore = "1.2.1"` /
  `androidx-datastore-preferences` and `app/build.gradle.kts` had
  `implementation(libs.androidx.datastore.preferences)`, while **nothing in the repository used it** —
  a declared dependency with zero consumers, which is the shape of a decision that was made and never
  carried out.
- `android:localeConfig` was declared and `res/xml/locales_config.xml` shipped, so Android 13+ offered
  a **system** per-app language picker — and the scaffold's language row opened that system screen
  (`Settings.ACTION_APP_LOCALE_SETTINGS` / app-details). The owner rejected that jump on 2026-10-05:
  language must be chosen **inside the app**. The factory standard is now the AndroidX AppCompat
  in-app picker (reference: `apps/2026-10-01-lawn-round-log/`).
- `scripts/core/` had no settings check at all, so every gate stayed green while the app shipped with
  nowhere to change anything. A defect no program reads is a defect that ships.

`scripts/core/settings.mjs` is the sensor, and it is the only thing that measures this rule.

## Rules

- **A settings surface exists, and it is a real destination.** A `@Composable` whose name ends in
  `Screen` or `Route` and names Settings/Preferences, declared under a `settings/` or `preferences/`
  package. Check id: **`settings.surface`**.
- **The surface is reachable from inside the app.** A navigation entry point (`NavDisplay` / `NavHost`
  / `NavEntry`) references it **and** something outside the settings files offers a way in — a
  settings-named route, destination or callback. A route key with no affordance is a screen that
  exists on disk and nowhere in the app. Check id: **`settings.reachable`**.
- **The language row opens an in-app picker via AppCompat.** The factory standard is
  `AppCompatDelegate.setApplicationLocales` hosted in an `AppCompatActivity`, with language tags read
  from `res/xml/locales_config.xml` (kept as the picker's data, not as a promise to the system). Prefer
  **not** declaring `android:localeConfig` on `<application>`: declaring it makes Android 13+ offer a
  second, system-side control for the same value, and the settings Language row must stay inside the
  app. The primary language row must **not** `startActivity` to `Settings.ACTION_APP_LOCALE_SETTINGS`
  or `ACTION_APPLICATION_DETAILS_SETTINGS` — that jump is the defect the owner named on 2026-10-05.
  Never reimplement the locale plumbing (`createConfigurationContext` / `applyOverrideConfiguration`):
  two writers for one language is how an app ends up in a language nobody chose. Reference slot:
  `apps/2026-10-01-lawn-round-log/`. Check id: **`settings.language`**.
- **A theme choice, wired into the theme host.** The settings model declares a light/dark/system
  preference and it reaches a host that declares or calls a `*Theme(…)` (or `isSystemInDarkTheme(…)`),
  so choosing it changes something. Following the system is the default and one of the options, not
  the only behaviour. Check id: **`settings.theme`**.
- **At least one preference changes what the app *does*.** A value that is not about language or
  appearance, read by app logic outside the settings surface and the data layer. A screen whose every
  toggle changes how the app looks and none of them what it does is half a settings screen.
  Check id: **`settings.behaviour`**.
- **Every preference survives the process.** `preferencesDataStore` in the data layer (the dependency
  is already declared, so this is its consumer), written through `edit { }` / `updateData { }`,
  consumed by a ViewModel that hands the screen a `StateFlow`, with the screen naming no `DataStore`
  type. A store declared inside the surface, or read directly by a composable, puts persistence in
  the UI layer where it cannot be tested or previewed. Check id: **`settings.persistence`**.
- **Every `R.string.<name>` the surface names is declared in the default resource file.** A reference
  to a string nobody declares does not compile, and a name declared only in `values-<locale>/` is
  Android's missing-default-resource error rather than a translation. This is the one string fact no
  other rule owns — literals belong to `locale.hardcoded`, locale trees to `slot.locales`.
  Check id: **`settings.strings`**.
- **The scaffold carries the surface before any slot does.** `scaffold/android-compose/` is what every
  new slot is copied from, so a finding there is the count of slots that would be born without
  settings. The scaffold has no grandfathering, and these rows are **expected to be red until the
  orchestrator wires the scaffold's own screen into `PlayKilnApp.kt`** — the wiring is the fix, and
  the check is never weakened to make it pass. Check ids: the seven above, run against the scaffold.
- **This rule and this module agree, or neither is trustworthy.** `settings.documented` fails if
  `.cursor/rules/app-settings.mdc` stops naming `scripts/core/settings.mjs` or stops naming a check id.
  A rule text that has drifted from its sensor is a rule nobody follows. Check id:
  **`settings.documented`**.
- **Applicability is derived, not hand-maintained.** The rule took effect **2026-09-30**. The slots
  that already exist carry their findings as a printed note rather than a permanent red row through
  `scripts/core/baseline.mjs` — a slot that is not a product (no `economics.json`), a killed slot, and
  a slot created before that date. Every slot created from now on is gated. A note is not a pass.

## What is required, and what is refused as padding

Three categories are required, because each is a question the user actually asks and each is
verifiable: **which language** (the in-app AppCompat picker), **light or dark** (the two palettes the
app already owns), and **at least one behaviour preference** that a real code path reads. Anything
beyond that has to earn its row.

Refused, with the reason this line is drawn:

- **`android:localeConfig` on `<application>` when the Language row is an in-app AppCompat picker.**
  Declaring it invites a second, system-side control for one value. Keep `res/xml/locales_config.xml`
  as the picker's language list; do not advertise it to the framework via `localeConfig`
  (<https://developer.android.com/guide/topics/resources/app-languages>; reference:
  `apps/2026-10-01-lawn-round-log/`).
- **A language row that opens `ACTION_APP_LOCALE_SETTINGS` / app-details.** That leaves the app for a
  device settings screen; the owner requires the picker to stay inside the app.
- **A stored language tag in DataStore when AppCompat / the framework already stores it.** AppCompat's
  `autoStoreLocales` (and on API 33+ the framework store) already persist the choice.
- **"Clear cache"**, when the slot has no cache. A control with no observable effect teaches the user
  that Settings is decoration.
- **A notifications toggle**, when the slot posts no notification and declares no `POST_NOTIFICATIONS`.
  A switch for a permission the app does not need is padding in the shape of a feature, and it
  contradicts the permission-minimisation baseline.
- **"Share"** as padding, and **"Rate this app" judged as a settings preference.** Rate is an action,
  not a preference this sensor owns — the In-App Review contract is `.cursor/rules/in-app-review.mdc`
  (`scripts/core/review.mjs`). This settings rule neither requires nor forbids the Rate row; it
  refuses treating Share/Rate as substitutes for a real behaviour preference.
- **Animation speed, developer options, or any debug switch**, which are not user preferences.
- **A settings search field for four groups.** Search earns its place in a long list; in a list of
  four rows it is noise that has to be translated forever.
- **A version/legal row that duplicates About.** The scaffold already has an About destination; two
  places showing one fact is two places to update and one to forget.
- **A privacy-policy row as a *settings* requirement.** The policy must be reachable from inside the
  app (`AGENTS.md`, Play compliance floor), but the settings screen is not required to be where it
  lives — that is a placement decision, not a preference.
- **A settings screen with no preference read outside it.** That is theatre, and it is what
  `settings.behaviour` exists to make unshippable.

## What is banned

- A settings surface that is a screen of switches nothing reads.
- A route to settings that no affordance opens, or a `NavEntry` key with nothing pointing at it.
- A language row whose primary action is `Settings.ACTION_APP_LOCALE_SETTINGS` /
  `ACTION_APPLICATION_DETAILS_SETTINGS`, `AppCompatDelegate.setApplicationLocales` in a host that is
  not an `AppCompatActivity`, an unguarded `LocaleManager.applicationLocales` write, or
  `createConfigurationContext` / `applyOverrideConfiguration` rebuilding the locale beside AppCompat.
- `preferencesDataStore(…)` declared inside the settings surface, a composable naming `DataStore`, or
  a store with no writer (which can only ever return its defaults).
- An `R.string.<name>` in a settings screen that no default resource file declares.
- A dynamic-colour switch below API 31. The platform feature does not exist there, so the row must be
  unavailable and say so rather than be a switch that silently does nothing
  (<https://developer.android.com/develop/ui/views/theming/dynamic-colors>).
- Weakening `settings.reachable` or the scaffold rows to make a wiring task look finished.
- A second check for hardcoded copy or for touch targets: those belong to `locale.hardcoded` and
  `uiux.a11y`. A duplicate is a second thing to drift, not extra safety.

## Boundary

This rule owns the **existence, reachability, contents and persistence** of the settings surface:
that there is a place to change language, appearance and behaviour, that the app offers a route into
it, that every preference it shows is read somewhere, and that the choices survive the process. It
does **not** own the copy pipeline (`locale.*`, `.cursor/rules/localization.mdc`), the touch-target
and accessible-name rules (`uiux.a11y`, `.cursor/rules/ui-ux-baseline.mdc`), the navigation host and
the IME/inset behaviour (`uiux.navigation`, `uiux.insets`, same rule), or the palette and identity of
the screen (`.cursor/rules/visual-identity.mdc`). Those produce the *shape* this surface is built
from; this rule must not restate them, and they must not restate the four shapes here.

The rows are shaped the way they are because of that boundary, not by preference: each row is one
focus target with the whole row clickable and at least 48 dp tall, and the accessibility decisions
are the ones the Compose accessibility guidance and `uiux.a11y` require
(<https://developer.android.com/develop/ui/compose/accessibility>), so this rule states the pattern
once and lets `uiux.a11y` measure it. The M3 list/switch/radio vocabulary is the published pattern
for a settings list (<https://m3.material.io/components/switch/overview>), and the settings route is
an ordinary key on the same back stack the host owns, so predictive back pops it like any other
destination (`.agents/skills/android-official/navigation-navigation-3/SKILL.md`; the list also has to
clear the system bars, `.agents/skills/android-official/system-edge-to-edge/SKILL.md`).

## How it is verified

```bash
node scripts/verify.mjs                 # the invariants group runs settingsFixtures() and settingsProjectChecks()
node scripts/verify.mjs --slot <slug>   # adds the ui group: the seven settings.* checks for one slot

# the sensor on its own, in the shape a reviewer can paste
node --input-type=module -e "import('./scripts/core/settings.mjs').then(m=>console.log(JSON.stringify(m.settingsFindings('apps/<slot>'),null,2)))"
```

- `scripts/core/settings.mjs` is the sensor. It reads Kotlin, XML and the build files **as source**,
  never pixels and never Gradle; it spawns no process.
- Findings are `file:line` strings, so a fix is a diff and not a reading. `settingsFindings()` is the
  whole set; `settingsAudit()` keeps them per check id.
- The check ids are `settings.surface`, `settings.reachable`, `settings.language`, `settings.theme`,
  `settings.behaviour`, `settings.strings` and `settings.persistence` (each run against one slot, and
  against the scaffold by `settingsProjectChecks()`), plus `settings.documented` for this file.
- `settingsFixtures()` runs in the `invariants` group and drives every check with a synthetic tree
  whose verdict is known — including the tree that must be clean, because a sensor that fires on
  everything is as useless as one that fires on nothing.
- The **DataStore** dependency is already declared, so this rule adds no dependency and needs no
  justification beyond its use; the published pattern it follows — store in the data layer, a `Flow`
  through a repository or ViewModel, `IOException` degraded to `emptyPreferences()` — is
  <https://developer.android.com/topic/libraries/architecture/datastore>.

## Deliberate under-approximations, named

A static scan that guesses produces false positives, and a noisy gate is read past. These are
**deliberately not taken**, and the reason is part of the rule:

- **It cannot prove a preference reaches the pixels.** `settings.theme` proves the value is read in a
  file that is a theme host; whether the resulting `ColorScheme` is used, or the switch looks right,
  is not visible statically.
- **A preference read by a branch that is never taken passes.** `settings.behaviour` proves the
  identifier is read outside the surface and the data layer, not that the read changes what the user
  sees. Pairing the read with an observable effect stays with the builder and the owner's review.
- **A UI-state mirror is not the settings model.** A `data class` inside a `settings/` package is used
  only when nothing outside such a package declares a settings value at all, so a screen holding its
  own copy of `themeMode` cannot stand in for a persisted setting. The inverse is accepted: a value
  declared in `data/` and never persisted would be missed, which is why `settings.persistence` asks
  about the store separately.
- **`settings.behaviour` cannot judge whether the behaviour is worth a setting.** It requires one
  non-appearance, non-language preference that something reads; naming the *right* one is a design
  decision, and the honest floor is one real one rather than a count.
- **Whether a row is a single accessible target is `uiux.a11y`'s finding, not this module's.** The
  screen is written so it is, and the sensor that measures it is not this one.
- **The strings check reads the default resource file, not what the screen renders.** A resource
  declared and never used, or a translated name with no default, is outside it.
- **Nothing here checks back-stack correctness or predictive back.** The settings route is an ordinary
  destination on the host's own back stack, and Navigation 3's own behaviour is measured by
  `uiux.navigation` and by running the app, not by this scan.
